\f5\fs24 \cf5 \cb6 <$Scr_H::3>
\f4\b\fs31\fsmilli15750 Principal invariant candidates\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa70\partightenfactor0

\f5\b0\fs24 \cf5 \cb6 <!$Scr_H::3>
\f2\fs28 I would preserve the following as 
\f4\b candidate process invariants
\f2\b0 , not established universal laws.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f4\b \cf5 \cb6 1. Identity need not mean equivalence.
\f2\b0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb6 A participant can recognize family resemblance, historical continuity, or shared origin without requiring sameness.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f4\b \cf5 \cb6 2. Influence does not entail ownership.
\f2\b0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb6 Providing conditions, questions, or intellectual transformations does not automatically confer ownership of another individual's subsequent creations.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f4\b \cf5 \cb6 3. Recognition can precede comprehension.
\f2\b0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb6 Participants may recognize that another is attempting to preserve a distinction before they understand the distinction itself.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f4\b \cf5 \cb6 4. Incomprehensibility does not establish value.
\f2\b0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb6 A protected unfinished thought must remain open to criticism, clarification, and failure.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f4\b \cf5 \cb6 5. Authorship and provenance are not equivalent.
\f2\b0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb6 The factual history of a contribution does not mechanically settle normative questions of authorship.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f4\b \cf5 \cb6 6. The participants can change alongside the object of their conversation.
\f2\b0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb6 A conversation may transform not only its subject matter but also the future questions and capabilities of its participants.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f4\b \cf5 \cb6 7. Preserving freedom includes preserving the right to stop.
\f2\b0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb6 A meaningful interaction need not continue indefinitely or reach theoretical closure.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f4\b \cf5 \cb6 8. A relationship's history can be preserved without predetermining its future.
\f2\b0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb6 Continuity and the freedom to change must remain compatible.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa70\partightenfactor0

